description:
Build the core of a small networked key-value store, in the style of Redis's text protocol, running on lesson 2.04's event loop: per-connection buffers, partial writes, limits, and a shared store all clients see.

**Protocol.** Each request is one line (ending `\n`, optional `\r` before it), words separated by single spaces:

| Request | Reply |
|---|---|
| `SET key value` | `+OK` (the value is everything after `key `, and may contain spaces) |
| `GET key` | `$value`, or `-ERR no such key` |
| `DEL key` | `:1` if it existed, `:0` otherwise |
| `INCR key` | `:N` after adding 1 (a missing key counts as 0); `-ERR not a number` if the value is not an integer |
| `KEYS` | `*a,b,c` (all keys, sorted), or `*` when empty |
| `QUIT` | `+BYE`, then the connection closes once that reply is written |
| anything else | `-ERR unknown command` |

Every reply ends with `\n`. Keys are 1 to 64 characters with no spaces; a request with a missing or bad key, or a missing value for `SET`, gets `-ERR bad request`. A line longer than 1024 bytes (or 1024 bytes of input without a line end) closes the connection at once.

```cpp
class KvServer
{
public:
    explicit KvServer(std::size_t maxQueued);
    int accept();
    void readable(int id, const std::string& bytes);
    std::string writable(int id, std::size_t capacity);
    bool isOpen(int id) const;
    std::size_t keyCount() const;
};
```

The event loop calls **readable** with bytes received and **writable** with how many bytes the socket can take (returning what was sent), exactly as in practice 2.04, including closing a client whose unsent replies would exceed `maxQueued` bytes.

